1
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:14,120
She’s taken over 255 flights.

2
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:28,840
She’s filled five passports, she’s planted trees, has spoken to students in over 40 different countries, and she’s funded all $110,000 through sponsors and investors.

3
00:00:29,720 --> 00:00:39,040
Now, her amazing adventure has finished and 29-year-old Cassie De Pecol has done it: she’s broken two Guinness World records.

4
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:54,346
After 18 months and 10 days, Cassie has become the fastest person to visit every country in the world, and the first woman on record to do so, in half the old record time.

5
00:00:55,040 --> 00:01:02,949
Back in 2014, Cassie decided to leave her job because she wasn’t doing what she really wanted to do.

6
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:10,380
What she really wanted to do was to see the world, and she started making plans for her great journey.

7
00:01:11,260 --> 00:01:19,640
She started saving all the money she could, and in July 2015, she left home to start her travels.

8
00:01:20,620 --> 00:01:33,983
Just over a year and a half later, on 2 February 2017, she arrived in Yemen, the 196th and last country on her (very!) long list.

9
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:41,703
Of course, she didn’t have much time to see each country, but she made sure to use her time well.

10
00:01:42,360 --> 00:01:46,266
She spent an average of two to five days in each country.

11
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:59,060
The best parts of her journey included: meeting local people, travelling to remote places on her own, planting trees and educating students on important world issues.

12
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:09,498
Cassie is a keen runner and triathlete and so she also didn’t enjoy staying in places where she didn’t feel safe enough to go for a run.

13
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:13,820
But Cassie’s journey was not just one long holiday.

14
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:16,160
She also wanted to make a difference.

15
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:24,869
She went as a Peace Ambassador (a special representative) for the International Institute for Peace Through Tourism.

16
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:35,086
In many of the countries she went to, she met and talked to local students – they discussed how tourism can be used to help each country.

17
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:47,960
Cassie is also very worried about the environment, so she agreed to help another organisation called Adventurers and Scientists for Conservation, funded by National Geographic.

18
00:02:48,860 --> 00:02:56,600
In many of the countries she went to, she collected samples of water for them to test for the presence of microplastics.

19
00:02:57,760 --> 00:03:00,106
Cassie has always wanted to travel.

20
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:04,260
When she was at school, she had a strong interest in other cultures.

21
00:03:05,020 --> 00:03:15,361
She was curious about how Americans have their origins in countries all over the world, and she wanted to find out more about where their ancestors came from.

22
00:03:16,260 --> 00:03:20,500
This journey was a chance to start answering some of these questions.

23
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:24,173
So, has she finished her travels now?

24
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:27,064
No, she hasn’t – not at all!

25
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:33,554
Cassie hasn’t become tired of travelling, and she is already making plans for her next journey.

26
00:03:33,554 --> 00:03:35,960
And she didn’t forget Antarctica!

27
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:40,080
She visited it on the last stop of her expedition.
